course: dictionary
lesson: 3831
title: Forsake: leaving and giving up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Forsake is an old-fashioned word. It means to leave someone and stop helping or caring for them. It can also mean to give up something you used to have or do. Old poems, hymns and stories use it, but today people usually say abandon, leave or give up.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «I will never leave you nor forsake you.» The words leave and nor tell you the two ideas go together. Forsake must mean something like leave, and it means never stop caring for someone.
explain: Forsake changes its shape, like many verbs. You can say forsake now, forsook in the past, and forsaken for a past form used with have or has, as in «why have you forsaken me?» Learn the three shapes, because they often appear together in old texts.
fact: Forsake means to leave someone and stop helping or caring for them.
fact: Forsake can also mean to give up something you used to have or do.
fact: Forsake is a formal, old-fashioned word, found in the Bible, hymns and poems.
fact: The past forms are forsook and forsaken.
fact: In everyday speech, people say abandon, leave or give up instead.
fact: «Forsaken» in «why have you forsaken me?» means left alone, with no one helping.
exercise: «The sailors feared the captain would forsake them in the storm.» What does forsake mean here? | Cheer them up with songs | *Leave them and stop helping them | Teach them to swim | Count the lifeboats
exercise: Which everyday word is closest in meaning to forsake? | *Abandon | Admire | Measure | Sing
exercise: Yesterday the cat ___ its old den. Which past form of forsake fits the gap? | forsaking | forsakes | *forsook | forsake
test: «I will never leave you nor forsake you.» What does forsake mean in this line? | *Never abandon you or stop caring for you | Never sing to you | Never forget your birthday | Never visit you
test: Jo decided to forsake sweets for a month. What does forsake mean here? | Share the sweets with friends | *Give up sweets | Count the sweets each day | Buy more sweets
exam: In «Christians remember that Jesus himself cried on the cross, «My God, why have you forsaken me?»», what does forsaken mean? | *Left alone, with no one helping | Happy and laughing with friends | Hungry after a long walk | Very cold and wet
